  ('N3.2-02', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'An owner spends $70,000 on a pool. In that neighborhood, pools add about $30,000 in value. Which principle explains why value rises only $30,000?', '["Contribution","Substitution","Conformity","Anticipation"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'An improvement is worth what it adds to value, not what it cost.', '["","Substitution is about equal alternatives.","Conformity is about similarity to the area.","Anticipation is about future benefits."]'::jsonb, 'Worth what it adds, not what it cost.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'contribution-pool', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.2-03', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'The largest, most expensive home in a neighborhood of small homes tends to lose value because of:', '["Progression","Contribution","Regression","Plottage"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'The best home among lesser ones is pulled down: regression.', '["Progression is the opposite: a modest home gains among better ones.","Contribution concerns specific improvements.","","Plottage is value from combining parcels."]'::jsonb, 'Biggest house on the block regresses.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'regression-biggest-house', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.2-04', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'A new freeway is built behind a neighborhood, and home values drop. This is:', '["Curable depreciation","Functional obsolescence","Physical deterioration","External obsolescence"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Loss from forces outside the property is external (economic) obsolescence.', '["The owner can''t fix an outside cause.","The design didn''t change.","Nothing about the house wore out.",""]'::jsonb, 'Wear, design, outside.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'external-obsolescence-freeway', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.2-05', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'A home has a bedroom you can reach only by walking through another bedroom. This is:', '["Physical deterioration","External obsolescence","Highest and best use","Functional obsolescence"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Outdated or poor design is functional obsolescence.', '["Nothing is worn out.","The cause is inside the property.","That''s a valuation principle, not a defect.",""]'::jsonb, 'Wear, design, outside.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'functional-obsolescence-bedroom', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.2-06', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'Buyers won''t pay more for a home than the cost of an equally desirable alternative. This is the principle of:', '["Regression","Contribution","Substitution","Conformity"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'Substitution underlies the sales comparison approach.', '["Regression is about the best home among lesser ones.","Contribution is about what improvements add.","","Conformity is about similarity."]'::jsonb, 'Why pay more for the same thing?', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'substitution-principle', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.2-07', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 1, 'recall', 'External obsolescence is usually incurable by the owner.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'True.', '["","The cause is outside the property and out of the owner''s control."]'::jsonb, 'Outside causes, out of your hands.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'external-incurable', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.2-08', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 2, 'recall', 'Under the principle of conformity, value is highest when a home is similar to the homes around it.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'True.', '["","Reasonable similarity supports value."]'::jsonb, 'Fit in to hold value.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'conformity-maximizes', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.2-09', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'A worn-out roof is an example of:', '["Functional obsolescence","Plottage","External obsolescence","Physical deterioration"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Wear and tear is physical deterioration.', '["The design isn''t the issue.","Plottage is about combining parcels.","The cause isn''t outside.",""]'::jsonb, 'Wear, design, outside.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'physical-deterioration-roof', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.2-10', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.2', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 3, 'application', 'A modest home in a neighborhood of larger, nicer homes tends to benefit in value. This is:', '["Regression","Substitution","Contribution","Progression"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Lesser homes among better ones are pulled up: progression.', '["Regression is the opposite.","Substitution is about alternatives.","Contribution is about specific improvements.",""]'::jsonb, 'Smallest house on a great block progresses.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'progression-small-house', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-01', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'Which approach is generally most reliable for valuing a single-family home?', '["Income","Sales comparison","Cost","Gross income multiplier"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'Homes trade often, so recent comparable sales are the best evidence.', '["Best for income properties.","","Best for new or special-use buildings.","Used for larger income properties."]'::jsonb, 'Homes are valued by what similar homes sold for.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'sales-comparison-single-family', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-02', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'In the sales comparison approach, adjustments are made to:', '["Neither","The subject property","The comparables","Both equally"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'Comps are adjusted to look like the subject.', '["Adjustments are essential.","The subject is the fixed point.","","Only comps are adjusted."]'::jsonb, 'Adjust the comp, never the subject.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'adjust-comp-not-subject', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-03', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 2, 'calculation', 'A comparable sold for $412,000 and has a garage worth $15,000. The subject has no garage. What is the comp''s adjusted price?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":397000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, 'Subtract the garage''s value from the better comp: $397,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Comp better, subtract. Comp worse, add.', 'Comp better than subject: adjusted price = comp price − value of difference', '["The comp has something the subject lacks, so subtract.","$412,000 − $15,000 = $397,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'comp-adjust-subtract', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-04', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 2, 'calculation', 'A comparable sold for $375,000. It has two bathrooms; the subject has three. An extra bathroom is worth $12,000. What is the comp''s adjusted price?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":387000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, 'Add to the inferior comp: $387,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Comp better, subtract. Comp worse, add.', 'Comp worse than subject: adjusted price = comp price + value of difference', '["The comp lacks a bathroom the subject has, so add.","$375,000 + $12,000 = $387,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'comp-adjust-add', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-05', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'Which comparable is most useful?', '["Sold 45 days ago, two blocks away, similar size and age","Currently listed next door","Sold last week, across town in a different school district","Sold three years ago, same street"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'The best comps are recent, nearby, and similar.', '["","A listing is an asking price, not a sale.","A different location can change value significantly.","Too old; the market may have changed."]'::jsonb, 'Recent, nearby, similar.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'best-comps-criteria', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-06', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'Which sale should usually be excluded as a comparable?', '["A sale two blocks away last month","A sale between a parent and child at a discount","A normal sale through the MLS","A sale with typical financing"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'Non-arm''s-length sales don''t reflect market value.', '["Recent nearby sales are ideal.","","Normal market sales are good comps.","Typical financing is fine."]'::jsonb, 'Family deals aren''t market deals.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'arms-length-comp', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-07', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 1, 'recall', 'If a comparable is better than the subject in some feature, the appraiser adds to the comp''s price.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'False.', '["If the comp is better, subtract the feature''s value to make it like the subject.",""]'::jsonb, 'Comp better, subtract.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'comp-better-subtract', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-08', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 3, 'calculation', 'A comp sold for $450,000. It has a pool worth $20,000 that the subject lacks, but it''s 200 sq ft smaller than the subject, at $100 per sq ft. What is the adjusted price?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":450000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, 'The two adjustments cancel out, leaving $450,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Comp better, subtract. Comp worse, add.', 'Adjusted price = comp price − superior features + inferior features', '["Pool: comp is better, subtract $20,000.","Size: comp is smaller, add 200 × $100 = $20,000.","$450,000 − $20,000 + $20,000 = $450,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'comp-multiple-adjustments', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-09', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'After adjusting several comps, the appraiser arrives at a single value by:', '["Using the highest one","Averaging all of them equally no matter what","Reconciling, giving more weight to the most similar comps","Using the lowest one"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'Reconciliation weighs the comps by how reliable and similar they are.', '["Picking the highest is biased.","Straight averaging ignores reliability.","","Picking the lowest is biased."]'::jsonb, 'Weigh the best comps most.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'reconciliation-comps', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.3-10', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.3', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'scenario', 2, 'application', 'Preparing a CMA, you find two recent nearby sales much lower than your seller hopes. What should the agent do?', '["Tell the seller they don''t count","Leave them out to keep the seller happy","Replace them with older, higher sales","Include them and explain how they affect the likely price"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Your client deserves an honest picture of the market.', '["Relevant sales do count.","Hiding relevant data misleads your client.","Cherry-picking undermines the analysis.",""]'::jsonb, 'Tell your client everything.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'agent-pick-comps-honest', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-01', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'The cost approach formula is:', '["Net operating income ÷ cap rate","Land value + cost of improvements new − depreciation","Monthly rent × GRM","Price of comp ± adjustments"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'The cost approach values land separately and depreciates the improvements.', '["That''s the income approach.","","That''s the GRM method.","That''s sales comparison."]'::jsonb, 'Land plus building new, minus wear.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'cost-approach-formula', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-02', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'Which property is best valued by the cost approach?', '["A public library","A tract home","A strip shopping center","A fourplex"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'Special-use properties rarely sell and don''t produce rent, so cost is the best evidence.', '["","Tract homes have plenty of comparable sales.","Retail centers are valued by income.","Rentals are valued by income."]'::jsonb, 'Rarely sold, rarely rented: use cost.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'cost-approach-special-use', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-03', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 2, 'calculation', 'Land is worth $90,000. The building would cost $320,000 to replace new and has $64,000 of total depreciation. What is the value by the cost approach?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":346000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, '$90,000 + $320,000 − $64,000 = $346,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Land plus building new, minus wear.', 'Value = land + replacement cost − depreciation', '["Depreciated building: $320,000 − $64,000 = $256,000.","Add land: $256,000 + $90,000 = $346,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'cost-approach-calc', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-04', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'The cost to build an exact replica of a historic building, using the same materials, is:', '["Replacement cost","Book value","Depreciated cost","Reproduction cost"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Reproduction builds an exact replica.', '["Replacement uses modern materials for the same usefulness.","Book value is an accounting figure.","Depreciation is subtracted from cost.",""]'::jsonb, 'Reproduction is a replica.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'reproduction-vs-replacement', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-05', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 3, 'calculation', 'A building costs $400,000 new and has a 50-year economic life. Using straight-line depreciation, how much has it depreciated after 12 years?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":96000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, '$8,000 a year for 12 years is $96,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Divide by life, multiply by age.', 'Annual depreciation = cost ÷ economic life; total = annual × years', '["$400,000 ÷ 50 = $8,000 per year.","$8,000 × 12 = $96,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'straight-line-depreciation', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-06', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'In the cost approach, which part is not depreciated?', '["Land","Fence","Driveway","Building"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'Land doesn''t wear out, so it isn''t depreciated.', '["","Fences wear out.","Improvements like driveways wear out.","Buildings wear out."]'::jsonb, 'Land is indestructible.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'land-not-depreciated', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-07', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 1, 'recall', 'Replacement cost uses modern materials to build a structure with the same usefulness.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'True.', '["","That''s the definition of replacement cost."]'::jsonb, 'Replacement is the equivalent.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'replacement-modern-materials', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-08', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 2, 'recall', 'The cost approach is most reliable for very old homes.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'False. It''s most reliable for new construction.', '["Estimating depreciation on old buildings is hard, so the cost approach is less reliable for them.",""]'::jsonb, 'Cost works best when it''s new.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'cost-approach-best-old-homes', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-09', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'A homeowner''s insurance policy usually focuses on:', '["The home''s assessed value","The original purchase price","The cost to rebuild the structure","The land value"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'Insurance covers rebuilding the improvements, not the land.', '["Assessed value serves taxation.","Purchase price includes land and old market conditions.","","Land isn''t destroyed by fire."]'::jsonb, 'Insure the building; the land survives.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'insurance-replacement-cost', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.4-10', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.4', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 2, 'calculation', 'A new 2,200 sq ft home costs $185 per square foot to build. What is the construction cost?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":407000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, '2,200 square feet at $185 is $407,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Area times rate.', 'Cost = area × cost per square foot', '["2,200 × $185 = $407,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'cost-per-sqft-calc', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-01', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 2, 'calculation', 'A duplex produces $36,000 a year in net operating income. Comparable properties sell at a 7.5% cap rate. What is its value?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":480000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, '$36,000 ÷ 0.075 = $480,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'IRV: Income ÷ Rate = Value.', 'Value = NOI ÷ cap rate', '["7.5% = 0.075.","$36,000 ÷ 0.075 = $480,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'cap-rate-value-calc', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-02', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 2, 'calculation', 'A property sold for $1,200,000 and has NOI of $78,000. What is the cap rate, in percent?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":6.5,"tolerance":0.001,"unit":"%"}'::jsonb, '$78,000 is 6.5% of $1,200,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'IRV: Income ÷ Value = Rate.', 'Cap rate = NOI ÷ value × 100', '["$78,000 ÷ $1,200,000 = 0.065.","0.065 × 100 = 6.5%."]'::jsonb, null, 'cap-rate-find-rate', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-03', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 3, 'calculation', 'A fourplex rents each unit for $1,500 a month. Vacancy and collection loss is 5%, and operating expenses are $21,600 a year. What is the NOI?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":46800,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, '$72,000 less $3,600 vacancy less $21,600 expenses is $46,800.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Gross, minus vacancy, minus expenses.', 'NOI = potential gross income − vacancy loss − operating expenses', '["Potential gross income: 4 × $1,500 × 12 = $72,000.","Vacancy: $72,000 × 0.05 = $3,600; effective gross income = $68,400.","NOI: $68,400 − $21,600 = $46,800."]'::jsonb, null, 'noi-calc-vacancy', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-04', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'Which is NOT an operating expense when calculating NOI?', '["Property taxes","Insurance","Repairs and maintenance","Mortgage payments"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Debt service isn''t an operating expense; NOI measures the property, not the financing.', '["Property taxes are operating expenses.","Insurance is an operating expense.","Repairs are operating expenses.",""]'::jsonb, 'NOI ignores the mortgage.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'mortgage-not-operating-expense', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-05', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 1, 'calculation', 'Similar rental homes sell for 150 times their monthly rent. A home rents for $2,100 a month. What''s its estimated value?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":315000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, '$2,100 × 150 = $315,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'GRM: price ÷ monthly rent.', 'Value = monthly rent × GRM', '["$2,100 × 150 = $315,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'grm-value-calc', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-06', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'If NOI stays the same and the cap rate rises, the property''s value:', '["Stays the same","Falls","Rises","Doubles"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'Value = NOI ÷ cap rate, so a higher cap rate means a lower value.', '["Changing the rate changes the value.","","A higher divisor gives a lower value.","Nothing suggests doubling."]'::jsonb, 'Cap rate up, value down.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'cap-rate-inverse-value', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-07', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'Which approach is primary for an apartment building?', '["Cost approach","Income approach","Sales comparison only","Assessed value"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'Investors buy income, so the income approach is primary for rentals.', '["Cost is for new or special-use properties.","","Comparable sales help, but investors buy income.","Assessed value isn''t an appraisal approach."]'::jsonb, 'Buy income, value by income.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'income-approach-for-income-property', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-08', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 2, 'recall', 'A gross income multiplier uses annual income, while a gross rent multiplier usually uses monthly rent.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'True.', '["","GIM is annual for larger properties; GRM is monthly for homes."]'::jsonb, 'GRM monthly, GIM yearly.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'gim-annual', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-09', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 1, 'recall', 'Vacancy and collection loss is added to potential gross income.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'False.', '["It''s subtracted, to reach effective gross income.",""]'::jsonb, 'Gross, minus vacancy, minus expenses.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'vacancy-subtract', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.5-10', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.5', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 2, 'calculation', 'A rental home sold for $288,000 and rented for $1,800 a month. What is its gross rent multiplier?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":160,"tolerance":0,"unit":""}'::jsonb, '$288,000 ÷ $1,800 = 160.', '[]'::jsonb, 'GRM: price ÷ monthly rent.', 'GRM = sale price ÷ monthly rent', '["$288,000 ÷ $1,800 = 160."]'::jsonb, null, 'grm-find-multiplier', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-01', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'A comparative market analysis is mainly used to:', '["Help a seller set a list price or a buyer decide on an offer","Replace the lender''s appraisal","Set property taxes","Determine insurance coverage"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'A CMA helps clients make pricing decisions.', '["","Lenders need appraisals.","Assessors set taxes.","Insurers use rebuild cost."]'::jsonb, 'CMA: the licensee''s pricing tool.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'cma-purpose', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-02', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'In a CMA, expired listings mostly tell you:', '["The appraised value","The exact value of the subject","Prices that were too low","Prices that were likely too high for the market"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Homes that didn''t sell often were overpriced.', '["Expireds aren''t appraisals.","No single data point sets exact value.","Low prices tend to sell, not expire.",""]'::jsonb, 'Solds show value. Expireds show too high.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'cma-expired-meaning', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-03', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'Active listings in a CMA show:', '["The competition and what sellers are asking","What buyers paid","The tax assessment","Proven market value"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'Actives are the competition; they show asking prices, not value.', '["","Only sold prices show what buyers paid.","Assessments come from the assessor.","Asking prices aren''t proven value."]'::jsonb, 'Actives show competition.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'active-listings-competition', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-04', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'A broker price opinion is:', '["A government valuation","An appraisal by a certified appraiser","A guarantee of sale price","A licensee''s estimate of likely selling price, not an appraisal"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'BPOs are price estimates by licensees, often for lenders.', '["It isn''t from the government.","BPOs aren''t appraisals.","Nothing guarantees a price.",""]'::jsonb, 'Never call a BPO an appraisal.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'bpo-not-appraisal', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-05', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 1, 'recall', 'A licensee may label a CMA as an "appraisal" if the comps were carefully chosen.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'False.', '["Only appraisers prepare appraisals. A CMA must not be called one.",""]'::jsonb, 'Never call a CMA an appraisal.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'cma-label', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-06', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'tf', 2, 'recall', 'Recently sold comparables are the strongest evidence of value in a CMA.', '["True","False"]'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, 'True.', '["","Sold prices show what buyers actually paid."]'::jsonb, 'Solds show value.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'sold-best-evidence', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-07', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'scenario', 2, 'application', 'Your CMA supports about $485,000, but the seller insists on listing at $560,000. What should the agent do?', '["Agree to $560,000 and promise it will sell","Lie that the market supports $560,000","Show the data, explain the risks of overpricing, and document your advice; the seller makes the final decision","Refuse to list at any price"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'Give honest advice backed by data, document it, and respect the client''s lawful decision.', '["Promising results you can''t support is misleading.","That''s misrepresentation.","","You can list it if the seller understands the risks."]'::jsonb, 'Advise with data; the seller decides.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'agent-seller-overprice', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-08', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'Three nearby comps sold at $250, $260, and $255 per square foot. The subject is 1,800 sq ft. A reasonable value estimate is about:', '["$510,000","$255,000","$405,000","$459,000"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '1,800 × $255 ≈ $459,000.', '["That''s too high.","That''s the price per square foot times 1,000.","That''s too low for $250–$260 per square foot.",""]'::jsonb, 'Area times price per square foot.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'price-per-sqft-cma', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-09', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 2, 'calculation', 'Three comps sold for $380,000 (1,900 sq ft), $396,000 (2,000 sq ft), and $361,000 (1,900 sq ft). What is their average price per square foot?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":196,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"dollars per sq ft"}'::jsonb, 'The three comps average $196 per square foot.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Price per square foot, then average.', 'Average = sum of each comp''s price ÷ its square feet, divided by number of comps', '["$380,000 ÷ 1,900 = $200.","$396,000 ÷ 2,000 = $198.","$361,000 ÷ 1,900 = $190.","($200 + $198 + $190) ÷ 3 = $196."]'::jsonb, null, 'cma-average-price-sqft', 'unverified'),
  ('N3.6-10', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N3', 'N3.6', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'A complete CMA typically includes:', '["Only tax records","Only active listings","Recent sales, active listings, and expired listings","Only the seller''s purchase price"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'Solds, actives, and expireds together show value, competition, and price ceilings.', '["Tax records aren''t enough.","Actives alone don''t show value.","","The seller''s old price isn''t market evidence."]'::jsonb, 'Solds, actives, expireds.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'cma-includes', 'unverified'),
  ('N4.1-01', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N4', 'N4.1', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 1, 'calculation', 'A buyer borrows $342,000 to buy a home priced and appraised at $380,000. What is the loan-to-value ratio, in percent?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":90,"tolerance":0,"unit":"%"}'::jsonb, '$342,000 is 90% of $380,000.', '[]'::jsonb, 'LTV: loan over value, lower value wins.', 'LTV = loan ÷ lower of price or appraised value × 100', '["$342,000 ÷ $380,000 = 0.90.","0.90 × 100 = 90%."]'::jsonb, null, 'ltv-calc-basic', 'unverified'),
  ('N4.1-02', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N4', 'N4.1', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'application', 'A home sells for $400,000 but appraises for $390,000. A lender offering 80% LTV will lend up to:', '["$320,000","$312,000","$390,000","$316,000"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '80% of the lower value, $390,000, is $312,000.', '["That uses the price; lenders use the lower of price or appraisal.","","That''s 100% of the appraisal.","That''s an average of the two values, which lenders don''t use."]'::jsonb, 'LTV: loan over value, lower value wins.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'ltv-lower-of-value', 'unverified'),
  ('N4.1-03', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N4', 'N4.1', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 1, 'calculation', 'A borrower pays 1.5 points on a $280,000 loan. How much do the points cost?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":4200,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, 'Each point is 1% of the loan, so 1.5 points is $4,200.', '[]'::jsonb, 'A point is 1% of the loan, not the price.', 'Points cost = loan amount × (points ÷ 100)', '["1.5 points = 1.5% = 0.015.","$280,000 × 0.015 = $4,200."]'::jsonb, null, 'points-cost-calc', 'unverified'),
  ('N4.1-04', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N4', 'N4.1', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'One point equals:', '["1% of the purchase price","1% of the down payment","1% of the loan amount","$1,000"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'A point is 1% of the loan amount.', '["Points are based on the loan, not the price.","The down payment isn''t the base.","","Points are a percentage, not a fixed dollar amount."]'::jsonb, 'A point is 1% of the loan, not the price.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'point-based-on-loan', 'unverified'),
  ('N4.1-05', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N4', 'N4.1', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 1, 'recall', 'Private mortgage insurance protects:', '["The borrower if they lose their job","The home against fire","The lender if the borrower defaults","The buyer against title defects"]'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, 'PMI protects the lender on higher-LTV conventional loans.', '["PMI doesn''t protect the borrower.","That''s homeowner''s insurance.","","That''s title insurance."]'::jsonb, 'Under 20% down? Insure the lender.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'pmi-purpose', 'unverified'),
  ('N4.1-06', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N4', 'N4.1', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'PMI is usually required on a conventional loan when the LTV is:', '["Any LTV on an FHA loan","Above 80%","Below 80%","Exactly 50%"]'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, 'Conventional loans above 80% LTV typically require PMI.', '["FHA loans use MIP, not PMI.","","Lower LTVs generally don''t require PMI.","50% LTV is low risk."]'::jsonb, 'Under 20% down? Insure the lender.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'pmi-over-80-ltv', 'unverified'),
  ('N4.1-07', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N4', 'N4.1', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'number', 1, 'calculation', 'A home is worth $465,000. The owner owes $298,000 on the first loan and $22,000 on a home equity loan. What is the owner''s equity?', '[]'::jsonb, '{"value":145000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dollars"}'::jsonb, 'Equity is value minus every loan secured by the property.', '[]'::jsonb, 'Value minus debt equals equity.', 'Equity = market value − all secured debt', '["Total debt: $298,000 + $22,000 = $320,000.","$465,000 − $320,000 = $145,000."]'::jsonb, null, 'equity-calc', 'unverified'),
  ('N4.1-08', 'lesson', null, false, 'national', 'N4', 'N4.1', 'Content Outlines 01/15/2025 (#092901) [verify]', 'PRIN', 'mcq', 2, 'recall', 'Why would a borrower pay discount points?', '["To avoid an appraisal","To shorten the closing period","To skip mortgage insurance","To lower the interest rate"]'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, 'Discount points are prepaid interest that buys down the rate.', '["Points don''t replace an appraisal.","Points don''t affect closing timing.","Points don''t eliminate mortgage insurance by themselves.",""]'::jsonb, 'Pay now, pay less later.', null, '[]'::jsonb, null, 'discount-points-lower-rate', 'unverified'),
